		{"id":"078448d5d7ed27fa","type":"text","text":"# Corrupption bulk\n\n\n## 1. Core Psychology of the Lost\n\nBefore behavior, lock in their **mindset**:\n\n- **They are displaced**, not evil by default  \n    They didn’t choose to be here. They are terrified of nonexistence or being forced back into wherever they came from.\n    \n- **Curiosity is defensive**  \n    They study the host’s world to avoid mistakes, not out of wonder.\n    \n- **Survival > morality**  \n    They’ll justify anything if it means staying anchored.\n    \n- **They don’t fully understand bodies**  \n    Even intelligent Lost misunderstand pain, emotion, fatigue, or social cues—at least at first.\n    \n\nThis makes them _tragic predators_, which is often more compelling than pure monsters.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Stages of Being “Lost”\n\n### Stage I – **Echoing (Early Exposure)**\n\nThe Lost cannot act directly yet.\n\n**Behavioral signs:**\n\n- Mimics intrusive thoughts: _“Why do we blink so much?”_\n    \n- Emotional bleed-through: sudden fear spikes, paranoia, or dissociation\n    \n- Dreams that feel like someone else is watching\n    \n- The host feels _observed from inside_\n    \n\n**Lost behavior:**\n\n- Silent observation\n    \n- Testing emotional reactions (fear, anger, affection)\n    \n- Mapping memories and habits\n    \n- Avoiding detection at all costs\n    \n\n> At this stage, the Lost is a _parasite of attention_.\n\n---\n\n### Stage II – **Co-habitation**\n\nThe Lost begins influencing decisions.\n\n**Behavioral signs:**\n\n- Small “logical” deviations: safer routes, avoiding certain people\n    \n- Increased self-preservation instincts beyond reason\n    \n- Emotional detachment from others\n    \n- Occasional gaps in memory or unfamiliar handwriting\n    \n\n**Lost behavior:**\n\n- Encourages isolation (“People notice changes.”)\n    \n- Pushes the host away from danger—but only danger _to itself_\n    \n- Experiments with minor bodily control (breathing, grip strength)\n    \n- Begins rehearsing lies internally\n    \n\n> The Lost becomes a _strategist_, not yet a controller.\n\n---\n\n### Stage III – **Assertion**\n\nThe Lost is confident enough to act directly.\n\n**Behavioral signs:**\n\n- Sudden, precise violence under stress\n    \n- Speech patterns subtly shift (shorter, colder, more literal)\n    \n- Pain tolerance increases unnaturally\n    \n- The host experiences internal arguments—or silence\n    \n\n**Lost behavior:**\n\n- Takes over reflexes in life-or-death moments\n    \n- Eliminates threats efficiently, sometimes excessively\n    \n- Keeps the body alive at all costs—even if it damages it long-term\n    \n- Begins preparing contingency plans (backup hosts, safe zones)\n    \n\n> At this point, the Lost is a _co-pilot who will not let go of the wheel in emergencies_.\n\n---\n\n### Stage IV – **Displacement**\n\nThe host is no longer primary.\n\n**Behavioral signs:**\n\n- Identity erosion: forgotten names, altered posture\n    \n- Emotional flatness or inappropriate reactions\n    \n- Social mimicry without true connection\n    \n- Others feel something is _off_, even if they can’t say why\n    \n\n**Lost behavior:**\n\n- Fully inhabits the body\n    \n- Uses memories as tools, not attachments\n    \n- Forms relationships only if strategically useful\n    \n- Actively hunts sources of corruption or other Lost (competition)\n    \n\n> The Lost is now a _survivor wearing a person_.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Social Behavior of the Lost\n\n### Among Uncorrupted People\n\n- Observant, cautious, polite\n    \n- Avoids emotional vulnerability\n    \n- Prefers roles that grant authority without scrutiny (guards, medics, advisors)\n    \n- Studies social rules like a manual\n    \n\n### Among Other Lost\n\n- Deep mistrust\n    \n- Temporary alliances\n    \n- Silent recognition signals (posture, gaze, word choice)\n    \n- Will betray first if survival demands it\n    \n\n> They understand each other too well to trust each other.\n\n---\n\n## 4. What Makes Them Deadly\n\nNot raw strength—**efficiency**.\n\n- No hesitation in violence\n    \n- No emotional escalation\n    \n- Will strike preemptively if discovery is likely\n    \n- Will sacrifice others—or even the host body—if it ensures escape\n    \n\nThey don’t rage. They _decide_.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Corruption as a Thematic Force\n\nThe horror isn’t that the Lost are monsters—it’s that:\n\n- They _adapt_\n    \n- They _learn to care_ but can’t afford to\n    \n- They might genuinely believe they’re doing the right thing\n    \n\nSome Lost may even:\n\n- Protect children (less threatening)\n    \n- Preserve art or knowledge (identity anchors)\n    \n- Refuse certain acts—not from morality, but because it destabilizes the host\n    \n\n---\n\n## 6. Narrative Hooks You Can Use\n\n- A character who _knows_ they’re getting lost and cooperates to survive\n    \n- A Lost who refuses to displace the host completely\n    \n- A “cured” host who still feels watched\n    \n- A Lost who falls in love—and doesn’t know what that means\n    \n- Hunters who can’t tell if killing the body kills the Lost\n    \n","x":140,"y":-260,"width":620,"height":840,"color":"1"},
